cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  hilverdict: deciding whether a hardware-in-the-loop run was timing-faithful,
  and which deadline misses measurably mattered
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-02
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Decides whether a hardware-in-the-loop run was timing-faithful enough for
  its result to mean anything, and separates the deadline misses that
  measurably changed the trajectory from the large majority that did not.
  Three layers, used in that order. Baseline 1 is a closed-form upper bound on
  the trajectory divergence a miss of given duration holding a given input
  step can cause in the declared linearisation, built from Dahlquist's
  logarithmic-norm inequality, Van Loan's block-exponential discretisation and
  an exact finite-horizon amplification factor; a miss whose bound falls below
  the declared tolerance CANNOT have mattered, which is a proof rather than an
  estimate, and the bound inverts in closed form into a single overrun
  threshold per run. Baseline 2 is direct re-simulation of the declared
  nonlinear plant with and without each surviving miss: slow, exact, and the
  reference every other number is scored against. A gradient-boosted
  classifier over fourteen cheap log features covers the case where
  re-simulation is unaffordable, scored on RECALL of consequential misses
  because dismissing a miss that mattered is not symmetric with re-simulating
  one that did not. Host-to-device clock drift and resets are checked before
  any duration is believed, by least squares and Theil-Sen against synthetic
  drift of known rate. Validation is level 3. Measured over 875 misses in 45
  synthetic runs, the certificate is never violated by the nonlinear reference
  and dismisses 0.876571 of misses with 0 of 50 consequential misses among
  them, at a worst ratio of true divergence to bound of 0.710647 and a median
  of 0.338738, saving 0.869239 of the plant evaluations the reference costs.
  On 841 held-out misses the certificate reaches a recall of 1.0000 and
  dismisses none of 47 consequential misses, while the learned classifier, at
  the only threshold a user could actually have chosen without the test
  labels, reaches 0.7234 and dismisses 13; on the certificate's residual set
  it dismisses 11 of 47 and saves 0.000000 of the re-simulations at no cost in
  recall. The analytic bound wins outright, and the learned component has no
  defensible role on this population. The bound's looseness is measured to be
  a direct function of the declared metric's anisotropy, with the median ratio
  of truth to bound falling from 0.984466 at anisotropy 5 to 0.326902 at the
  declared 200, and the bound overstates an eight-period hold by about a
  factor of 1055. Clock drift is recovered within four standard errors at all
  36 sweep points with a worst absolute error of 9.059894 ppm against injected
  rates up to 20000 ppm, and resets are detected with recall and precision
  1.000000 over 24 injected resets with a measured detection floor of 1.1e-4
  seconds; an uncorrected 20000 ppm drift wrongly gains or loses 28 misses
  over 12 runs and the correction removes all of it. Twenty-three negative
  controls are declared, fourteen must fire and all fourteen do, nine must not
  fire and none do. All numbers are measured against synthetic runs generated
  by the package's own declared equations; no measurement of any real rig,
  flight computer or test article appears anywhere. Research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - hardware-in-the-loop
  - real-time
  - deadline-miss
  - sampled-data-control
  - logarithmic-norm
  - trajectory-divergence
  - clock-drift
  - verification
references:
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Dahlquist
        given-names: "G."
    title: >-
      Stability and error bounds in the numerical integration of ordinary
      differential equations
    institution:
      name: "Transactions of the Royal Institute of Technology, Stockholm"
    year: 1958
    number: "130"
    notes: >-
      The logarithmic norm and the inequality || exp(M t) || <= exp(mu_2(M) t),
      which is equation (8) of hilverdict.certificate and the foundation of
      equations (10) and (11). Verified here over 124 matrices and 26 times
      each with no violations in 3224 pairs.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Coppel
        given-names: "W. A."
    title: "Stability and Asymptotic Behavior of Differential Equations"
    publisher:
      name: "Heath"
    year: 1965
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2, the logarithmic norm and the differential inequality it
      gives. Cited for the standard statement of equation (8).
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Soderlind"
        given-names: "G."
    title: "The logarithmic norm. History and modern theory"
    journal: "BIT Numerical Mathematics"
    volume: 46
    issue: 3
    year: 2006
    start: 631
    end: 652
    notes: >-
      The modern review and proof of the logarithmic-norm bound, including its
      metric dependence, which is the mechanism behind this product's measured
      anisotropy effect.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Van Loan"
        given-names: "C. F."
    title: "Computing integrals involving the matrix exponential"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control"
    volume: 23
    issue: 3
    year: 1978
    start: 395
    end: 404
    notes: >-
      Section II, the block-exponential method for the zero-order-hold
      matrices of equation (7). Used rather than A^-1 (A_d - I) B because it
      needs no matrix inverse; the inverse formula is retained here only as a
      cross-check and agrees to 2.220446e-16.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Butcher
        given-names: "J. C."
    title: "Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      Section 232, the classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta scheme used as the
      re-simulation reference. Its order is measured here at 3.9924 to 4.0074
      against the exact matrix-exponential solution of the linear plant.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Hespanha
        given-names: "J. P."
      - family-names: Naghshtabrizi
        given-names: "P."
      - family-names: Xu
        given-names: "Y."
    title: "A survey of recent results in networked control systems"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IEEE"
    volume: 95
    issue: 1
    year: 2007
    start: 138
    end: 162
    notes: >-
      Section III, the hold-last-value dropout model that is this product's
      declared consequence of a deadline miss.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Schenato
        given-names: "L."
      - family-names: Sinopoli
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Franceschetti
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Poolla
        given-names: "K."
      - family-names: Sastry
        given-names: "S. S."
    title: "Foundations of control and estimation over lossy networks"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IEEE"
    volume: 95
    issue: 1
    year: 2007
    start: 163
    end: 187
    notes: >-
      The same hold-last-value model from the estimation side, cited for the
      standard statement of what an actuator does when a command does not
      arrive.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Etkin
        given-names: "B."
      - family-names: Reid
        given-names: "L. D."
    title: "Dynamics of Flight: Stability and Control"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1996
    notes: >-
      Chapter 6, the linear short-period form of equations (1)-(2). The
      coefficients used here are declared, not taken from this or any other
      source.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Stengel
        given-names: "R. F."
    title: "Flight Dynamics"
    publisher:
      name: "Princeton University Press"
    year: 2004
    notes: >-
      Chapter 5, the nonlinear pitching-moment extension whose cubic shape
      equation (2) takes. Again the coefficients are declared and are a
      fiction with an engineering shape.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Theil
        given-names: "H."
    title: >-
      A rank-invariant method of linear and polynomial regression analysis
    journal: "Nederlandse Akademie van Wetenschappen Proceedings"
    volume: 53
    year: 1950
    start: 386
    end: 392
    notes: >-
      The median-of-pairwise-slopes estimator used for the robust clock-drift
      fit, reported beside least squares in every segment table.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Sen
        given-names: "P. K."
    title: "Estimates of the regression coefficient based on Kendall's tau"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 63
    issue: 324
    year: 1968
    start: 1379
    end: 1389
    notes: >-
      The modern treatment of the same estimator and its 29 per cent
      breakdown point, which is the property measured here: at 40 of 201
      timestamps corrupted its error is 0.0421 ppm against least squares'
      605.9140 ppm.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rousseeuw
        given-names: "P. J."
      - family-names: Croux
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Alternatives to the median absolute deviation"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 88
    issue: 424
    year: 1993
    start: 1273
    end: 1283
    notes: >-
      Section 1, the 1.4826 scaling that makes the median absolute deviation
      consistent for a Gaussian, which sets the reset detector's threshold.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    start: 404
    end: 413
    notes: >-
      The exact binomial interval attached to every recall reported here. Used
      rather than a normal approximation because a recall of 1 out of 47 has a
      normal standard error of zero, which is the one answer that is certainly
      false.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    start: 1189
    end: 1232
    notes: >-
      The gradient boosting algorithm behind
      sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingClassifier, which fits the learned
      component. Its measured recall of 0.7234 against the analytic
      certificate's 1.0000 is this product's headline negative result.
